{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>So Jeshurun grew fat and kicked.</b> They were not called Jeshurun until now, when Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, called them by this name. <b>And it is plausible</b> to explain that he called them thus for their deficiency, in order to reprove them and shame them, for this name is a name of greatness and honor, derived from (Numbers 24) אשורנו, on account of the vision they saw — the glory of the Shekhinah eye to eye at the giving of the Torah, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 24:17): ״ומראה כבוד ה' כאש אוכלת בראש ההר״ — and therefore after he mentioned the giving of the Torah, where he said יסובבנהו יבוננהו, he immediately called them by the name Jeshurun; and so below, after he mentioned (Deuteronomy 33:5) תורה צוה לנו, he immediately said ויהי בישרון מלך; and also at the conclusion of his blessing he said (ibid.) אין כאל ישרון, that is, from what you saw at Sinai you can testify about Him that there is none like Him. And all this is part of the words of this Song, which are words of reproof and instruction — he was reproving them and making known to them the future, how they are destined to sin, and that they will not keep the mouth of HaShem and will forget His many kindnesses that He did with them, and therefore he called them on that day three times by this honored name, for the sections are contiguous, and it all was on the day of his death, as I have mentioned. And now he says: וישמן ישרון ויבעט, to hint to them that a people such as this, called Jeshurun, who saw the glory of the Shekhinah, will kick against Him and abandon and act wickedly. And similar to this said Isaiah, peace be upon him (Isaiah 1:4): הוי גוי חוטא עם כבד עון, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: גוי — a nation called (Deuteronomy 4:8) ומי גוי גדול — that it should be חוטא; עם — a people called (ibid. 14) כי עם קדוש אתה — that it should be כבד עון; זרע — seed regarding whom it is written (Isaiah 61:9) זרע ברך ה' — that they should be מרעים; בנים — sons who were called (Deuteronomy 14:1) בנים אתם לה' אלהיכם — that they should be משחיתים." "<b>He forsook the God who made him, and spurned the Rock of his salvation.</b> The verse hints at the two attributes by which we went forth from Egypt: he did not say ויטוש אל but אלוה, in order to include grandeur and honor, and therefore he added ו״ו ה״א, and this is (Job 35:10): איה אלוה עושי; and צור ישועתו — that through Him was the salvation at the sea."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :register {:flags {1 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:20.583419889Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}